  invariant registrar migration parses namespaced native XML, preserves TXT content and MX priorities and TTLs, refuses unsupported record semantics, reads copied Cloudflare rows back and rechecks the source before explicit nameserver mutation; credentials do not appear in argv or output
  witness scripts/tests/dns-migration.test.mjs runs the actual shell entrypoint with mocked APIs and native libxml decoding, proves read-only default, exact account/domain guards, record copy readback, source-drift refusal and no registrar mutation after a failed copy; Clang static analysis of foreign_xml.c is clean
  known_gap DNS migration acknowledgement is not DNS propagation, DNSSEC rollover, or preservation of registrar forwarding services. Unsupported record types are refused rather than silently discarded; NAMECHEAP_API_USER and IPv4 NAMECHEAP_CLIENT_IP must be explicitly configured. No live DNS migration was run for this retirement.
  source_path scripts/tests/cloudflare-entrypoints.test.mjs
  invariant tunnel startup and cache purge share the Bun/CUE reconciliation owner; startup gives the connector only its scoped run token, and exact-path purge resolves every unique authored zone before any invalidation
  forbidden_inference mocked connector startup or accepted purge envelope -> a deployed healthy tunnel or fresh content at every edge
  witness scripts/tests/cloudflare-entrypoints.test.mjs exercises the real shell entrypoints without Python or live network, preserves quoted and Unicode purge paths, refuses ambiguous zones and upstream failures, strips administrative credentials from connector environment, forwards termination signals, and propagates child exit status
  source_path scripts/reconcile_cloudflare_public_roots.py.archive
  source_path scripts/reconcile_cloudflare_tunnel.py.archive
  witness scripts/tests/cloudflare-reconcile.test.mjs proves the shared native reconciler preserves territory-specific member bindings, dry-run non-mutation, foreign Worker refusal, explicit estate Worker replacement, and unrelated Tunnel ingress
  witness scripts/tests/placement_plane_test.sh compiles the full declaration and proves canonical MCP/Auth origins are different, the three legacy Worker exclusions survive, the ordered Tunnel plan sends both control hosts to Oracle before the work wildcard, Supabase receives only sealed OAuth transaction continuity plus an atomic consume function, and the failure matrix preserves last proven public material when Oracle is unavailable
  witness integrations/mcp/tests/oauth_institution_slice_test.sh proves protected-resource metadata advertises mcp.fixture.test while authorization-server metadata advertises auth.fixture.test, preserving separate resource and issuer identities through a real OAuth/MCP gateway fixture
  witness scripts/tests/publish_r2_route_aliases_test.sh proves the publisher projects every HTML route to the same extensionless R2 object key and excludes non-page files; an incomplete subset is not treated as route coverage
  witness evidence/placement-plane/bootstrap-2026-09-23.json records the live Supabase continuity migration and Cloudflare managed Tunnel ingress acceptance, while explicitly retaining the denied Worker-route/DNS writes and down Oracle connector as un-applied rather than laundering them into deployment success
  witness 2026-09-27 auth.aurekai.life, auth.aurekai.live, auth.aurekai.shop and signin.aurekai.work each served the native account HTML, authenticated self-profile fragment and shared HTMX runtime through a healthy four-connection managed Tunnel; each public territory apex redirected member paths to its own sibling while the static Worker continued serving public material
  witness 2026-09-27 after the free Worker request budget returned 429 on all four public apexes, aurekai.life, aurekai.live, aurekai.shop and aurekai.work each returned 200 through exact no-Worker root routes and proxied Tunnel CNAMEs; the live Life apex completed native signup, login, authenticated account and HTMX runtime reads while wildcard subject routes were not changed
  witness 2026-10-03 every aurekai host answered Cloudflare 1033 because the managed Tunnel had no connector: its last connection was 2026-09-28 and the Oracle tenancy held no compute instance at all. The connector, the address organ and the OAuth bridge were restarted on the operator's machine from deploy/launchd; the four public apexes returned 200, aurekai.work served authorization-server and protected-resource metadata with the apex as issuer and resource, the authorize page rendered for a ChatGPT connector request, and an unauthenticated POST to /mcp returned 401 with its resource-metadata pointer. No authorization flow was completed
  witness scripts/tests/cloudflare-reconcile.test.mjs proves the reconciler splits the gateway paths off the apex ahead of its catch-all, leaves a path-scoped rule's service alone, and matches only the seven exact paths
  interacts provider-graph
  interacts placement-graph
  interacts address-plane
  interacts cmd-auth
  interacts spatial-observation
  source_lineage Oracle to Cloudflare/Supabase runtime-placement recovery
  known_gap The public origin is one laptop. The three units were loaded for the login session from deploy/launchd and are not installed as login agents, so a restart or a network loss on that machine takes every host down again, which is how they went down on 2026-09-28
  known_gap The OAuth bridge on the operator's machine reads the local copy of the auth store, not the one that lived on the Oracle estate; an account that existed only there cannot sign in, and a connector's earlier tokens are gone
  known_gap The four member-session siblings are live and separately witnessed, but the canonical OAuth/MCP control-plane hosts still need their own end-to-end authorization-flow witness. The direct material R2 lane remains a bounded material realization rather than a replacement for a public territory Worker.
